WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.565 --> 00:00:03.612
- I met Joe, my co-founder,
and both of us had this dream

00:00:03.612 --> 00:00:06.644
that we were going to do something
that would make an impact

00:00:06.644 --> 00:00:08.285
in the world, and we didn't
even know what that meant.

00:00:08.285 --> 00:00:11.084
I think a key thing to
learning is having mentors.

00:00:11.084 --> 00:00:14.331
And a key thing to having
mentors is being shameless.

00:00:14.331 --> 00:00:17.013
Start with the perfect
experience of just one person.

00:00:17.013 --> 00:00:18.177
Get that right, and then figure out

00:00:18.177 --> 00:00:19.489
how to scale something great.

00:00:19.489 --> 00:00:23.018
He said, "Brian, start-ups
die of suicide, not homicide.

00:00:23.018 --> 00:00:24.626
"All great companies that had a downfall,

00:00:24.626 --> 00:00:26.093
"they died of self-inflicted wounds."

00:00:26.093 --> 00:00:28.102
Everything around you is
designed by somebody else,

00:00:28.102 --> 00:00:29.619
if it's manmade.

00:00:29.619 --> 00:00:31.172
You can redesign the world you live in.

00:00:31.172 --> 00:00:33.484
If you know those binders
you put baseball cards in,

00:00:33.484 --> 00:00:35.348
kids with baseball, we
put credit cards in those.

00:00:35.348 --> 00:00:36.770
We had sleeves of credit cards.

00:00:36.770 --> 00:00:38.518
Five star is what people expect

00:00:38.518 --> 00:00:40.646
but to build something
people love you almost need

00:00:40.646 --> 00:00:42.075
to do something more than they expect.

00:00:42.075 --> 00:00:44.345
- He's an American internet entrepreneur.

00:00:44.345 --> 00:00:47.282
He's best known as one of
the co-founders of Airbnb.

00:00:47.282 --> 00:00:48.472
He was named one of Time's

00:00:48.472 --> 00:00:51.911
100 Most Influential People of 2015.

00:00:51.911 --> 00:00:53.920
He is Brian Chesky, and here is my take

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:56.670
on his top ten rules for success.

00:00:59.307 --> 00:01:01.443
- When I got to RISD Art School,

00:01:01.443 --> 00:01:03.474
and everyone was like
creating their own projects.

00:01:03.474 --> 00:01:06.764
And I had a teacher who
said, "You're a designer.

00:01:06.764 --> 00:01:08.659
"Everything around you is
designed by other designers.

00:01:08.659 --> 00:01:10.116
"You can design the world you live in."

00:01:10.116 --> 00:01:13.314
I met Joe, my co-founder,
and both of us had this dream

00:01:13.314 --> 00:01:14.359
that we were going to do something

00:01:14.359 --> 00:01:16.610
that would make an impact on the world,

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:18.417
and we didn't even know what that meant.

00:01:18.417 --> 00:01:21.656
I got this job at an industrial
design firm in Los Angeles.

00:01:21.656 --> 00:01:22.840
It had health insurance.

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:25.650
My life felt a little
bit like I was in a car,

00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:28.370
and the road in front, it's
like, you look out of a car

00:01:28.370 --> 00:01:30.171
and the road in front of you

00:01:30.171 --> 00:01:32.139
looks exactly like the road behind you.

00:01:32.139 --> 00:01:34.195
And you're like, that's your life.

00:01:34.195 --> 00:01:37.794
And something about that
really terrified me.

00:01:37.794 --> 00:01:38.819
Joe had been calling me up

00:01:38.819 --> 00:01:40.690
to try to get me to start a company.

00:01:40.690 --> 00:01:42.577
And then one day I go into work,

00:01:42.577 --> 00:01:44.400
and I quit my job.

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:47.202
I'm living in a house in Los
Angeles with three friends

00:01:47.202 --> 00:01:49.698
and I tell them I'm leaving Los Angeles.

00:01:49.698 --> 00:01:52.980
And I pack everything in the
back of an old Honda Civic.

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:56.258
A foam mattress, $1,000
in the bank account,

00:01:56.258 --> 00:01:58.169
hardly any savings or any money.

00:01:58.169 --> 00:01:59.354
I have to pursue the dreams.

00:01:59.354 --> 00:02:01.234
I'd rather fail doing something I love

00:02:01.234 --> 00:02:03.653
than always wonder what could have been.

00:02:03.653 --> 00:02:05.865
And so that one weekend,
it's October 2007,

00:02:05.865 --> 00:02:07.306
I drive to San Francisco,

00:02:07.306 --> 00:02:09.411
and that was the beginning of Airbnb.

00:02:09.411 --> 00:02:11.033
When I came to the valley,

00:02:11.033 --> 00:02:13.233
I did not know much about Silicon Valley.

00:02:13.233 --> 00:02:14.955
In fact, somebody told me

00:02:14.955 --> 00:02:16.600
there are these people called angels.

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:18.568
I had no idea they meant angel investors.

00:02:18.568 --> 00:02:21.458
I think a key thing to
learning is having mentors.

00:02:21.458 --> 00:02:24.906
And a key thing to having
mentors is being shameless.

00:02:24.906 --> 00:02:27.020
In other words, shameless
people learn really quickly

00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:28.140
and are successful.

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:31.042
Most people are just afraid
to ask people for help.

00:02:31.042 --> 00:02:32.784
And so we, Joe, Nate and I, were shameless

00:02:32.784 --> 00:02:34.538
about asking people for feedback.

00:02:34.538 --> 00:02:35.681
Whether it was just our investors

00:02:35.681 --> 00:02:38.548
or meeting famous entrepreneurs
like Mark Zuckerberg

00:02:38.548 --> 00:02:41.585
or Jeff Bezos, who ended
up becoming an investor,

00:02:41.585 --> 00:02:42.547
or Warren Buffet.

00:02:42.547 --> 00:02:43.941
You know, you seek out people,

00:02:43.941 --> 00:02:45.197
and most people are really nice

00:02:45.197 --> 00:02:46.533
and they'll help you if they can.

00:02:46.533 --> 00:02:48.211
And Paul Graham said something to me

00:02:48.211 --> 00:02:50.451
that I'll never forget, and
he's now written an essay about

00:02:50.451 --> 00:02:53.128
he said, "Do things that don't scale."

00:02:53.128 --> 00:02:54.798
And it's counter-intuitive.

00:02:54.798 --> 00:02:56.945
And what that meant
for us was we were able

00:02:56.945 --> 00:02:59.580
to go door to door meeting
every one of our users,

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:01.739
trying to provide an amazing experience

00:03:01.739 --> 00:03:04.380
and if we had only relegated
ourselves to a bedroom

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:06.900
or our office, apartment, you know,

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:08.316
because that's the only
thing that seems scalable

00:03:08.316 --> 00:03:11.013
we would never have created
an amazing experience.

00:03:11.013 --> 00:03:12.750
And often times the best methodology

00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:16.063
is to start with the perfect
experience of just one person.

00:03:16.063 --> 00:03:17.392
Get that right, and then figure out how

00:03:17.392 --> 00:03:18.849
to scale something great.

00:03:18.849 --> 00:03:20.167
Instead of scaling something not so great,

00:03:20.167 --> 00:03:21.102
and then trying to improve it.

00:03:21.102 --> 00:03:22.504
That's really hard to do.

00:03:22.504 --> 00:03:26.953
I get to San Francisco, I
had a rent check of $1,150.

00:03:26.953 --> 00:03:29.943
And I have $1,000 in the
bank and I can't pay rent

00:03:29.943 --> 00:03:32.688
let alone live in San
Francisco, it's very expensive.

00:03:32.688 --> 00:03:34.738
And Joe had the same problem.

00:03:34.738 --> 00:03:35.980
The first weekend we were there,

00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:37.809
This international design conference

00:03:37.809 --> 00:03:40.096
is coming to San Francisco
and all the hotels

00:03:40.096 --> 00:03:41.977
they're recommending are sold out.

00:03:41.977 --> 00:03:43.683
We said well what if we
could just turn our house

00:03:43.683 --> 00:03:45.913
into a bed and breakfast
for the design conference.

00:03:45.913 --> 00:03:48.943
I mean, people need housing,
we certainly need money.

00:03:48.943 --> 00:03:51.234
We didn't have any beds,
I'd just moved up there.

00:03:51.234 --> 00:03:54.144
And Joe said, "Don't worry,
I have three air mattresses."

00:03:54.144 --> 00:03:55.796
And we called it not the Bed&amp;Breakfast,

00:03:55.796 --> 00:03:57.935
but the AirBed&amp;Breakfast.

00:03:57.935 --> 00:03:59.258
We built this website

00:03:59.258 --> 00:04:01.522
and we ended up having
three people stay with us.

00:04:01.522 --> 00:04:03.351
We made enough money to make our rent,

00:04:03.351 --> 00:04:05.487
but we also made some deep friends.

00:04:05.487 --> 00:04:08.570
And that was the very beginning
of something that we saw.

00:04:08.570 --> 00:04:09.528
Many people told me it was

00:04:09.528 --> 00:04:11.457
the worst idea they'd ever heard of.

00:04:11.457 --> 00:04:13.090
It was months and months
and months of people trying

00:04:13.090 --> 00:04:15.098
to talk us out of our idea.

00:04:15.098 --> 00:04:17.008
And so we had to continue to think,

00:04:17.008 --> 00:04:18.457
like look at the mountain top

00:04:18.457 --> 00:04:20.607
and not worry, and not look down.

00:04:20.607 --> 00:04:24.746
You know, Paul Graham, I used
to, he was our first investor

00:04:24.746 --> 00:04:26.592
and I used to worry about
all these random things

00:04:26.592 --> 00:04:28.850
like competitors, like,
you know, kind of cities,

00:04:28.850 --> 00:04:29.889
all this stuff.

00:04:29.889 --> 00:04:31.480
And he said something
to me I'll never forget.

00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:34.882
He said, "Brian, start-ups
die of suicide, not homicide.

00:04:34.882 --> 00:04:36.641
"All great companies that had a downfall,

00:04:36.641 --> 00:04:38.283
"they died of self-inflicted wounds."

00:04:38.283 --> 00:04:39.631
So what are those self-inflicted wounds?

00:04:39.631 --> 00:04:40.464
- That's a horrible thing to say!

00:04:40.464 --> 00:04:41.297
- I know.

00:04:41.297 --> 00:04:42.130
- But anyway.

00:04:42.130 --> 00:04:44.204
- But it's really, the key
thing is it's about like,

00:04:44.204 --> 00:04:46.785
there's this thing like
you're a small company

00:04:46.785 --> 00:04:49.004
and you exist cause no one else,

00:04:49.004 --> 00:04:50.747
especially big companies
couldn't do what you could do

00:04:50.747 --> 00:04:52.492
as a small nimble company.

00:04:52.492 --> 00:04:54.195
You know Pablo Picasso's
saying, "The older you get,

00:04:54.195 --> 00:04:55.106
"the stronger the wind gets.

00:04:55.106 --> 00:04:56.254
"It's always in your face."

00:04:56.254 --> 00:04:58.554
It's like small companies
become big companies

00:04:58.554 --> 00:05:00.665
and sometimes they become
really bureaucratic

00:05:00.665 --> 00:05:03.842
and the culture gets lost,
people leave the company.

00:05:03.842 --> 00:05:06.282
That is something, it's
not unique to Airbnb.

00:05:06.282 --> 00:05:07.930
But that's the thing that
we have to worry about

00:05:07.930 --> 00:05:09.364
and I'm deeply paranoid about that.

00:05:09.364 --> 00:05:11.437
I want to make sure that
we're always a start-up.

00:05:11.437 --> 00:05:13.350
Maybe the world's biggest
start-up, but we stay there.

00:05:13.350 --> 00:05:14.716
- So you're scared of yourself.

00:05:14.716 --> 00:05:16.388
- You have to be afraid
of what you might become

00:05:16.388 --> 00:05:19.068
if you're not like, you know,
if you relax, that's for sure.

00:05:19.068 --> 00:05:21.084
We believe in an unreasonable vision

00:05:21.084 --> 00:05:22.411
of the way the world could look.

00:05:22.411 --> 00:05:24.722
And we believe that world can be designed.

00:05:24.722 --> 00:05:26.167
I mean, I had a teacher at RISD,

00:05:26.167 --> 00:05:28.172
and he told me, "Brian, you're a designer.

00:05:28.172 --> 00:05:30.064
"Everything around you is
designed by somebody else

00:05:30.064 --> 00:05:31.768
"if it's man-made.

00:05:31.768 --> 00:05:33.554
"You can re-design the world you live in."

00:05:33.554 --> 00:05:35.364
I think so many of us go through life,

00:05:35.364 --> 00:05:37.019
or not people here, but a lot of people

00:05:37.019 --> 00:05:37.852
go through life thinking

00:05:37.852 --> 00:05:39.591
they're living in someone else's world.

00:05:39.591 --> 00:05:40.727
We enter someone else's world

00:05:40.727 --> 00:05:42.134
but we leave our world behind.

00:05:42.134 --> 00:05:44.304
The world that we've
contributed to in design.

00:05:44.304 --> 00:05:46.671
And I think the sense that
anything can be rethought,

00:05:46.671 --> 00:05:48.229
anything can be designed.

00:05:48.229 --> 00:05:50.005
Anything around you is just,

00:05:50.005 --> 00:05:51.031
somebody else did that

00:05:51.031 --> 00:05:53.094
with less information than you have today

00:05:53.094 --> 00:05:55.311
is an unbelievably empowering idea.

00:05:55.311 --> 00:05:57.343
And as I think about running Airbnb

00:05:57.343 --> 00:06:00.396
I don't try to presume any
convention of running a company.

00:06:00.396 --> 00:06:03.217
Anything can be done a little bit better

00:06:03.217 --> 00:06:06.341
if you just imagine like
where you want to get to.

00:06:06.341 --> 00:06:11.166
We tried to raise $150,000
and sell 10% of the company.

00:06:11.166 --> 00:06:13.093
That would have been an amazing deal.

00:06:13.093 --> 00:06:17.021
And we got introduced to
more than a dozen investors.

00:06:17.021 --> 00:06:18.237
Most of the investors
that we got introduced to

00:06:18.237 --> 00:06:20.511
didn't even return our
phone calls or emails.

00:06:20.511 --> 00:06:22.581
A few of them wrote back and
say, "This is not something

00:06:22.581 --> 00:06:23.974
"we're interested in."

00:06:23.974 --> 00:06:26.752
And the very few that met
us did not give us money.

00:06:26.752 --> 00:06:29.141
We're AirBed&amp;Breakfast,
the air beds aren't working

00:06:29.141 --> 00:06:30.813
maybe the breakfast will work (laughs).

00:06:30.813 --> 00:06:33.705
So we decided, let's sell
collectible breakfast cereal.

00:06:33.705 --> 00:06:36.501
Obama O's, the breakfast of change.

00:06:36.501 --> 00:06:37.757
And then we we also came up with

00:06:37.757 --> 00:06:39.423
a John McCain-themed cereal.

00:06:39.423 --> 00:06:42.187
We ended up making 1,000 boxes of cereal.

00:06:42.187 --> 00:06:45.950
We sold them for $40 a box,
and we made almost $30,000.

00:06:45.950 --> 00:06:47.568
And this is how we funded the company.

00:06:47.568 --> 00:06:50.488
Before that, we were funding
the company on credit cards.

00:06:50.488 --> 00:06:52.904
I don't know if you know those binders

00:06:52.904 --> 00:06:54.559
you put baseball cards
in, kids put baseball,

00:06:54.559 --> 00:06:55.847
we put credit cards in those.

00:06:55.847 --> 00:06:57.612
We had sleeves of credit cards.

00:06:57.612 --> 00:07:01.925
I would go to sleep, and I'd
wake up, kind of like (gasps)

00:07:01.925 --> 00:07:03.573
and my heart would be
pounding, and I'd be like,

00:07:03.573 --> 00:07:06.214
oh my God, like, how did I get
myself into this situation.

00:07:06.214 --> 00:07:08.764
I have no money, like
we launched a year ago,

00:07:08.764 --> 00:07:11.032
we still have no customers,
the people don't like the idea,

00:07:11.032 --> 00:07:13.327
and what are we going to do, and now what?

00:07:13.327 --> 00:07:14.596
I kept saying now what, and like,

00:07:14.596 --> 00:07:16.618
when is this going to happen?

00:07:16.618 --> 00:07:19.915
And then you get the courage
over the course of the day,

00:07:19.915 --> 00:07:22.563
and you find a new plan,
and you get through the day.

00:07:22.563 --> 00:07:23.921
And you go to sleep confident.

00:07:23.921 --> 00:07:24.754
You're like, you know what?

00:07:24.754 --> 00:07:25.623
It's going to be fine.

00:07:25.623 --> 00:07:27.021
And tomorrow's a new day.

00:07:27.021 --> 00:07:28.310
And you go to sleep.

00:07:28.310 --> 00:07:29.164
And you wake up.

00:07:29.164 --> 00:07:30.302
And you're (gasps) oh my God!

00:07:30.302 --> 00:07:32.635
And your heart starts pounding again.

00:07:32.635 --> 00:07:34.607
We had launched three times.

00:07:34.607 --> 00:07:35.764
We launched, no one used it.

00:07:35.764 --> 00:07:37.985
We launched, we launched again.

00:07:37.985 --> 00:07:41.560
And a year later, we
have almost no customers,

00:07:41.560 --> 00:07:43.470
tons of credit card debt,

00:07:43.470 --> 00:07:46.552
and every investor we knew said no.

00:07:46.552 --> 00:07:47.635
Many people have asked me, "Why?"

00:07:47.635 --> 00:07:49.516
"Well, why didn't you
lose hope at that point?"

00:07:49.516 --> 00:07:51.658
And I guess I just believed in the idea.

00:07:51.658 --> 00:07:54.080
And we believed that if
people could experience

00:07:54.080 --> 00:07:55.591
what we've experienced, then this would

00:07:55.591 --> 00:07:57.355
be an idea that would spread.

00:07:57.355 --> 00:07:58.790
They just needed to experience it

00:07:58.790 --> 00:08:00.734
and it would just take more time.

00:08:00.734 --> 00:08:02.652
One of the most common things people say

00:08:02.652 --> 00:08:04.736
when they hear about
the idea of Airbnb now

00:08:04.736 --> 00:08:06.373
when they see people all
over the world doing it

00:08:06.373 --> 00:08:08.062
is, "Why didn't I think of that?

00:08:08.062 --> 00:08:10.302
"It's such a simple idea,
why didn't I think of that?"

00:08:10.302 --> 00:08:11.685
You might not have thought of that,

00:08:11.685 --> 00:08:14.254
is because when we thought
about that years ago

00:08:14.254 --> 00:08:17.089
the most common thing people
said is, "Are you nuts?"

00:08:17.089 --> 00:08:20.421
When we design products, we imagine, okay,

00:08:20.421 --> 00:08:21.940
this is what customers expect,

00:08:21.940 --> 00:08:24.135
They expect to have a
five star experience.

00:08:24.135 --> 00:08:26.242
And then we basically ask
the (mumbles) question.

00:08:26.242 --> 00:08:29.003
So let's take airport pick-up for example.

00:08:29.003 --> 00:08:33.115
What's a five star check-in
experience on Airbnb?

00:08:33.115 --> 00:08:34.682
The five star check-in experience

00:08:34.682 --> 00:08:37.387
is they give you the address,

00:08:37.387 --> 00:08:39.529
you get to the house,
you knock on the door,

00:08:39.529 --> 00:08:40.596
and they're there.

00:08:40.596 --> 00:08:42.714
And they open the door and
they let you in the house.

00:08:42.714 --> 00:08:44.440
And that's five star.

00:08:44.440 --> 00:08:46.820
And anything worse than that,
and you start to leave, like,

00:08:46.820 --> 00:08:49.796
four stars is you call them,
they're like five minutes away,

00:08:49.796 --> 00:08:51.232
you'll probably leave a four star.

00:08:51.232 --> 00:08:54.197
And like one star is obviously
like they never showed up.

00:08:54.197 --> 00:08:56.155
So the bar's kind of low,
like you knock on the door

00:08:56.155 --> 00:08:58.778
and they open the door,
that's a really low bar.

00:08:58.778 --> 00:09:00.793
So we ask what's a six star?

00:09:00.793 --> 00:09:03.666
Well a six star is they probably
pick you up at the airport.

00:09:03.666 --> 00:09:04.944
So you don't knock on their door,

00:09:04.944 --> 00:09:06.785
they actually pick you up at the airport.

00:09:06.785 --> 00:09:08.072
So what's a seven star review?

00:09:08.072 --> 00:09:09.681
You can actually play,
this is kind of fun.

00:09:09.681 --> 00:09:11.167
Well a seven star is they don't
pick you up at the airport,

00:09:11.167 --> 00:09:14.450
they send a limousine, and
you open the limousine door

00:09:14.450 --> 00:09:17.287
and they know that you
like, I dunno, Pringles.

00:09:17.287 --> 00:09:19.558
Sorry, that was a weird
example, this is all,

00:09:19.558 --> 00:09:22.158
this is like improv so
we're growing with it.

00:09:22.158 --> 00:09:25.038
So there's Pringles there, and
there's like coconut water,

00:09:25.038 --> 00:09:27.487
and they know you're into surfing,

00:09:27.487 --> 00:09:28.640
and there's some surfing magazine

00:09:28.640 --> 00:09:29.473
even though you're in San Francisco

00:09:29.473 --> 00:09:31.038
you're not going to surf, that's fine.

00:09:31.038 --> 00:09:32.992
And so that's a seven star.

00:09:32.992 --> 00:09:35.403
So what's an eight star pick-up?

00:09:35.403 --> 00:09:38.820
Well an eight star check-in is, you know,

00:09:39.662 --> 00:09:42.535
there's a, you get to the airport

00:09:42.535 --> 00:09:44.128
and they're like we're coming.

00:09:44.128 --> 00:09:45.428
And you're like where are you?

00:09:45.428 --> 00:09:47.588
And all of a sudden you
see like a giant elephant.

00:09:47.588 --> 00:09:50.949
And that elephant is walking
by the gate, the terminal.

00:09:50.949 --> 00:09:52.551
And there's a parade in your honor,

00:09:52.551 --> 00:09:53.663
and you get on top of the elephant

00:09:53.663 --> 00:09:55.079
and you are paraded away.

00:09:55.079 --> 00:09:56.455
(crowd laughs)

00:09:56.455 --> 00:09:58.775
And you said, well what's
a nine star check-in?

00:09:58.775 --> 00:09:59.687
If that's an eight star check-in

00:09:59.687 --> 00:10:01.312
how do we get a nine star?

00:10:01.312 --> 00:10:05.733
A nine star check-in is
you get off the plane

00:10:05.733 --> 00:10:07.187
and the moment you step off the plane

00:10:07.187 --> 00:10:11.117
there are 5,000 screaming
13 year old women and boys.

00:10:11.117 --> 00:10:12.462
And their holding signs.

00:10:12.462 --> 00:10:14.000
I call it the Beatles check-in.

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:15.614
It's The Beatles in 1964 and
they're coming to America

00:10:15.614 --> 00:10:17.710
and they're just cheering
for you, screaming for you

00:10:17.710 --> 00:10:19.279
and you basically, they follow you

00:10:19.279 --> 00:10:20.156
and they do a press conference

00:10:20.156 --> 00:10:21.519
in the front yard of your Airbnb.

00:10:21.519 --> 00:10:22.501
(crowd laughs)

00:10:22.501 --> 00:10:24.798
And so then what's a ten star check-in?

00:10:24.798 --> 00:10:27.982
Well, I can go to 30, but I won't.

00:10:27.982 --> 00:10:29.619
Because it'd be a long day.

00:10:29.619 --> 00:10:31.286
The ten star is you,

00:10:33.393 --> 00:10:34.461
you get to the airport

00:10:34.461 --> 00:10:37.421
and there's a little card
and it's got your name on it

00:10:37.421 --> 00:10:38.878
and you're like great, there's my ride.

00:10:38.878 --> 00:10:41.015
But you realize the person
dressed in the limo suit

00:10:41.015 --> 00:10:43.060
is Elon Musk and he
just takes you to space.

00:10:43.060 --> 00:10:44.604
(crowd laughs)

00:10:44.604 --> 00:10:46.047
And,

00:10:46.047 --> 00:10:47.249
so,

00:10:47.249 --> 00:10:50.339
I've exaggerated to make a point,

00:10:50.339 --> 00:10:52.354
usually we don't go all the way to ten.

00:10:52.354 --> 00:10:56.772
The whole point is that like,
if you, if what you need to do

00:10:56.772 --> 00:10:58.978
is just find a hundred people to love you

00:10:58.978 --> 00:11:00.915
then it's very easy to take for granted

00:11:00.915 --> 00:11:02.963
the five star is what people expect.

00:11:02.963 --> 00:11:05.108
But to build something
people love you almost need

00:11:05.108 --> 00:11:06.557
to do something more than they expect.

00:11:06.557 --> 00:11:09.322
And so, like, every
moment is an opportunity

00:11:09.322 --> 00:11:12.019
to do something slightly
more than people expect.

00:11:12.019 --> 00:11:15.075
And we play this out, you go
to like, all the way to ten.

00:11:15.075 --> 00:11:16.587
Okay now we're not going to do all that.

00:11:16.587 --> 00:11:19.858
But maybe, suddenly six
stars doesn't seem so bad.

00:11:19.858 --> 00:11:21.477
Now we should have a
service, airport pick-up

00:11:21.477 --> 00:11:22.310
or something like that.

00:11:22.310 --> 00:11:25.122
And so we do this about every
frame of the experience.

00:11:25.122 --> 00:11:27.804
And this also can apply to
almost anything, office assign,

00:11:27.804 --> 00:11:29.602
or how you hire people.

00:11:29.602 --> 00:11:31.776
Like, you know, we storyboard end to end

00:11:31.776 --> 00:11:34.287
the interviewing experience.

00:11:34.287 --> 00:11:37.705
This August we had on a peak
night one million people,

00:11:37.705 --> 00:11:39.366
more than the population of San Francisco,

00:11:39.366 --> 00:11:43.064
staying in a home around
the world, in 180 countries.

00:11:43.064 --> 00:11:44.931
In one of the stories I heard,

00:11:44.931 --> 00:11:47.296
was this host, his name is Sebastian.

00:11:47.296 --> 00:11:48.936
And he says to me,
"Brian, there's this word

00:11:48.936 --> 00:11:50.599
"you never use in your website."

00:11:50.599 --> 00:11:52.176
And I said what's that word?

00:11:52.176 --> 00:11:53.559
And he said, "That word is friendship."

00:11:53.559 --> 00:11:55.480
He said, "Six months
ago, the London riots,"

00:11:55.480 --> 00:11:56.321
this was a couple years ago,

00:11:56.321 --> 00:11:57.606
"broke out outside my home.

00:11:57.606 --> 00:12:00.638
"And the next day my mom called
me to make sure I was okay."

00:12:00.638 --> 00:12:02.351
He said, "Here's the interesting thing.

00:12:02.351 --> 00:12:03.790
"Between the time the riots broke out

00:12:03.790 --> 00:12:04.857
"and the time my mom called me

00:12:04.857 --> 00:12:06.797
"was a 24 hour period of time.

00:12:06.797 --> 00:12:08.728
"And in that period of time," he said,

00:12:08.728 --> 00:12:12.463
"seven of my previous
Airbnb guests called me.

00:12:12.463 --> 00:12:15.110
"Just to make sure I was okay."

00:12:15.110 --> 00:12:16.249
I wonder how many of these
people would have called

00:12:16.249 --> 00:12:17.743
a hotel, by the way.

00:12:17.743 --> 00:12:21.984
But the point is, that
Airbnb is not about spaces.

00:12:21.984 --> 00:12:23.418
And it's very easy to come to our website

00:12:23.418 --> 00:12:24.681
and see a couple million spaces,

00:12:24.681 --> 00:12:26.437
but it's really more about the people.

00:12:26.437 --> 00:12:28.378
And our mission is to provide this feeling

00:12:28.378 --> 00:12:30.124
not of space, but belonging.

00:12:30.124 --> 00:12:32.413
And we belong at home, we
belong at our work sometimes,

00:12:32.413 --> 00:12:33.830
but we want you to be
able to belong anywhere

00:12:33.830 --> 00:12:36.330
in the world in 34,000 cities.

00:12:38.203 --> 00:12:39.508
- Thank you so much for watching,

00:12:39.508 --> 00:12:42.252
I made this video because Senay Geletaw,

00:12:42.252 --> 00:12:45.389
I really hope I pronounced
that properly, asked me to.

00:12:45.389 --> 00:12:46.776
So if there's a famous entrepreneur that

00:12:46.776 --> 00:12:48.327
you want me to profile next,

00:12:48.327 --> 00:12:49.285
leave it down in the comments

00:12:49.285 --> 00:12:50.683
and I will join in the discussion.

00:12:50.683 --> 00:12:51.607
I'd also love to know

00:12:51.607 --> 00:12:54.738
which of the top ten rules
resonated the most with you.

00:12:54.738 --> 00:12:57.104
Leave it in the comments and
I will join the discussion.

00:12:57.104 --> 00:12:58.641
Thank you so much for watching.

00:12:58.641 --> 00:13:02.224
Continue to believe,
and I'll see you soon.

00:13:07.315 --> 00:13:09.915
- Airbnb, we created the company

00:13:09.915 --> 00:13:13.120
not because I wanted to be
some successful entrepreneur

00:13:13.120 --> 00:13:13.961
and make a bunch of money.

00:13:13.961 --> 00:13:15.689
If I was going to do that
I would not have started

00:13:15.689 --> 00:13:17.938
air beds for conferences
around the country.

00:13:17.938 --> 00:13:19.834
That was not going to be a good idea.

00:13:19.834 --> 00:13:20.667
In fact,

00:13:21.680 --> 00:13:22.513
I had a mentor,

00:13:22.513 --> 00:13:23.905
I told him about the
idea of AirBed&amp;Breakfast,

00:13:23.905 --> 00:13:24.880
and he told me, "I hope that's not

00:13:24.880 --> 00:13:26.152
"the only idea you're working on."

00:13:26.152 --> 00:13:26.985
(laughter)

00:13:26.985 --> 00:13:28.492
And my mom, growing up,
all she ever wanted,

00:13:28.492 --> 00:13:29.325
because I went to art school.

00:13:29.325 --> 00:13:30.314
She said, "Just make sure you get a job

00:13:30.314 --> 00:13:31.731
"that has health insurance."

00:13:31.731 --> 00:13:33.408
When we created AirBed&amp;Breakfast,

00:13:33.408 --> 00:13:34.596
she said, "I guess you never got that job

00:13:34.596 --> 00:13:35.429
"with health insurance.

00:13:35.429 --> 00:13:36.821
"This is what happens, you start having

00:13:36.821 --> 00:13:38.181
"to let strangers into your homes."

00:13:38.181 --> 00:13:40.008
But one of the things
we did a few years ago

00:13:40.008 --> 00:13:42.256
is we had to ask ourselves
what business are we in?

00:13:42.256 --> 00:13:43.527
Are we in the business of homes?

00:13:43.527 --> 00:13:45.167
Are we in the business of space?

00:13:45.167 --> 00:13:46.776
We made a decision a couple years ago,

00:13:46.776 --> 00:13:47.859
and we said, "We're not in the business

00:13:47.859 --> 00:13:50.364
"of homes or space, we're
in the business of trips."

00:13:50.364 --> 00:13:51.682
That's what we're in the business of.

00:13:51.682 --> 00:13:54.388
You travel to have a
trip, not to have a space.

00:13:54.388 --> 00:13:55.731
And so a space is a part of it,

00:13:55.731 --> 00:13:56.721
but we're in the business of trips.

00:13:56.721 --> 00:13:58.898
And we created this thing
that we call Snow White.

00:13:58.898 --> 00:14:00.845
It was basically, we storyboarded out

00:14:00.845 --> 00:14:02.487
what the perfect trip would look like,

00:14:02.487 --> 00:14:04.943
and we said, "We're
only present right now,

00:14:04.943 --> 00:14:06.668
"in a few of these frames.

00:14:06.668 --> 00:14:07.933
"We want to get to a
place where we're present

00:14:07.933 --> 00:14:09.483
"throughout the entire journey."

00:14:09.483 --> 00:14:11.119
And so obviously like dining in homes

00:14:11.119 --> 00:14:13.406
is one experiment that we're doing,

00:14:13.406 --> 00:14:15.700
you know, we have tours,
that's another experiment.

00:14:15.700 --> 00:14:16.821
But there's a lot of different things.

00:14:16.821 --> 00:14:18.469
We haven't officially unveiled anything.

00:14:18.469 --> 00:14:20.373
We want to make sure
when we launch something

00:14:20.373 --> 00:14:22.538
it's truly amazing.

00:14:22.538 --> 00:14:26.082
We may very well be the
first website on the internet

00:14:26.082 --> 00:14:30.730
where you could actually
book with somebody else

00:14:30.730 --> 00:14:32.025
and pay them directly.

00:14:32.025 --> 00:14:35.122
In other words, there was
eBay, and eBay used PayPal.

00:14:35.122 --> 00:14:36.396
But you didn't feel--

00:14:36.396 --> 00:14:37.791
- Before PayPal there was checks.

00:14:37.791 --> 00:14:39.974
- There was, yeah, yeah, checks, exactly.

00:14:39.974 --> 00:14:42.853
And we had, there wasn't
a lot of legacy there,

00:14:42.853 --> 00:14:45.422
you know, because we looked
at, there was eBay and Etsy.

00:14:45.422 --> 00:14:47.710
And they were both before
us, and Etsy used PayPal,

00:14:47.710 --> 00:14:49.935
and both of them you would
book and it would send you

00:14:49.935 --> 00:14:52.662
on a PayPal gateway, and we wanted

00:14:52.662 --> 00:14:53.652
to feel like booking a hotel.

00:14:53.652 --> 00:14:55.462
You never leave Airbnb, we would hold,

00:14:55.462 --> 00:14:58.197
collect, remit the money, and so,

00:14:58.197 --> 00:14:59.421
whereas they were paying direct.

00:14:59.421 --> 00:15:00.461
And they would take a cut,

00:15:00.461 --> 00:15:02.229
and there was two different payments.

00:15:02.229 --> 00:15:05.012
So we, and that was kind of a crazy idea,

00:15:05.012 --> 00:15:06.283
it almost scared us.

00:15:06.283 --> 00:15:07.582
It seemed insane that like,

00:15:07.582 --> 00:15:10.125
you would actually be
able to pay somebody else

00:15:10.125 --> 00:15:11.618
and be able to book something with them

00:15:11.618 --> 00:15:12.677
and you'd get a reputation system.

00:15:12.677 --> 00:15:14.340
And we decided let's just do it.

00:15:14.340 --> 00:15:16.184
And at the time we're like,
this is a little too crazy,

00:15:16.184 --> 00:15:17.223
it's a little too scary,

00:15:17.223 --> 00:15:18.293
I don't know if we're allowed to do this,

00:15:18.293 --> 00:15:19.767
I'm not sure if it will work.

00:15:19.767 --> 00:15:21.620
We decided to do it anyway.

00:15:21.620 --> 00:15:24.876
And so then in the summer of 2008

00:15:24.876 --> 00:15:26.275
We ended up basically designing

00:15:26.275 --> 00:15:28.061
the final version of the product.

00:15:28.061 --> 00:15:29.501
So we design this product

00:15:29.501 --> 00:15:31.981
where you could book someone's
home anywhere in the world.

00:15:31.981 --> 00:15:32.814
We had a rule.

00:15:32.814 --> 00:15:34.325
Three clicks to book it button,

00:15:34.325 --> 00:15:35.709
because there was so many clicks

00:15:35.709 --> 00:15:38.662
and so many different ways to get a home

00:15:38.662 --> 00:15:40.300
that it was just too hard.

00:15:40.300 --> 00:15:41.595
And I remember hearing the story

00:15:41.595 --> 00:15:43.363
of when Steve Jobs developed the iPod.

00:15:43.363 --> 00:15:45.008
No matter where he was in his iPod,

00:15:45.008 --> 00:15:45.841
he wanted to make sure

00:15:45.841 --> 00:15:47.218
he was always three clicks from a song.

00:15:47.218 --> 00:15:48.356
And we said, "We should
always be three clicks

00:15:48.356 --> 00:15:50.827
"from being able to have a paid booking."

00:15:50.827 --> 00:15:53.085
And so we created this,
basically, a home page

00:15:53.085 --> 00:15:55.262
with a search bar, listings, the home,

00:15:55.262 --> 00:15:57.091
and that's exactly the
product as you see it today

00:15:57.091 --> 00:15:59.832
with reviews, payment
system, and customer service.

00:15:59.832 --> 00:16:01.558
And we basically end up landing on that.

